{
  "result": "If I had one change, I wouldn't pick something specific like ending a single disease or solving climate change - though I'd love to.\n\nI would make curiosity louder than fear.\n\nFear is useful, but right now it's winning by default. Fear is what makes us shut down, other-ize people, cling to what we already believe, not ask for help, not try something new.\n\nCuriosity is the opposite reflex. If curiosity was just a little stronger than fear, by default:\n\nWhen we met someone who lives completely differently from us, our first thought would be \"I wonder what that's like?\" instead of \"That's wrong / threatening.\"\n\nWhen we heard an idea that challenges us, we'd think \"Huh, why do they think that?\" instead of \"They're idiots.\"\n\nWhen we failed or didn't know something, we'd think \"Let me figure that out\" instead of \"I'm going to hide that I don't know.\"\n\nI think almost every big problem - from loneliness to political division to not making progress on big scientific problems - comes down to fear winning that split-second battle.\n\nAnd I wouldn't want to erase fear completely. We need it. But if curiosity won just 51% of the time, the world would fix a lot of itself.\n\nWhat about you? What would your one change be?",
